# Insurance Financial Analytics Dashboard (IFAD)

Three design concepts built from the Financial Dashboard PRD, all sharing the
same derived data and chart/table components.

## Routes

- `/ifad` — gallery index linking to the three concepts (`IfadGallery`)
- `/ifad/conservative` — clean, standards-first Carbon layout (`IfadConservative`)
- `/ifad/mild` — accented cards + portfolio donut + line/bar toggle (`IfadMild`)
- `/ifad/wild` — gradient hero, animated count-up metrics, asymmetric layout (`IfadWild`)

Routes are registered in `src/App.jsx`.

## Data

`src/utils/financialAnalytics.js` derives everything from the existing
`src/data/businessMockData.js`:

- `getFinancialSummary(mode)` — KPI figures (owed, claimed, net, loss ratio) and
  Auto/Property split. `mode` is `'gross'` or `'net'` (net subtracts approved
  claim payouts).
- `getMonthlySeries(mode)` — 12-month multi-series history for the chart
  (Property/Auto Premiums and Claims). Claims are bucketed by `incidentDate`.
- `getAssetLedger()` — combined Auto + Property rows with `premiumDue`,
  synthetic `dueDate`, and lifecycle `totalClaims`.
- `getAssetDetailPath(assetId)` — drill-down route (reuses existing
  `/business/properties/:id` and `/business/fleet/:id` detail pages).
- `SERIES_META` — stable label/color metadata for chart series.

## Shared components (`src/components/ifad/`)

- `IfadChart` — custom SVG multi-series chart. Props: `data`, `type`
  (`'line'` | `'bar'`). Series toggle on/off via the legend; hover shows a
  tooltip. No chart library dependency. Colors come from `SERIES_META` design
  tokens so it is theme-aware.
- `IfadLedger` — sortable Carbon `DataTable` ("Asset Performance Ledger").
  Numeric/date values are stored raw so sorting (e.g. Highest Claims, Due Date)
  is correct, and formatted on render. Rows drill down to asset detail.

## Conventions

- All styling uses design tokens / CSS variables (no hardcoded colors) and works
  in both light and dark themes, per the global CSS rules.
- Financial color semantics: green for premiums/revenue, red for claims/expenses.
- The "wild" variant's gradient hero intentionally uses fixed dark brand tokens
  (`--color-grey-90/100`, brand red/blue) for an immersive band; text inside is
  light for contrast in both themes.
